WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:04.685
BRETT HESTERBERG:
Good afternoon.

00:00:04.685 --> 00:00:07.110
We're about one second past
noon, so good afternoon

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.344
everyone.

00:00:08.344 --> 00:00:09.510
My name is Brett Hesterberg.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.150
I'm the product
manager for Cloud SQL.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.890
I'm joined today
by Stanley Feng.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.250
Stanley is the technical lead
for our Cloud SQL performance

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.540
engineering team.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.080
And we're talking today about
performance and availability

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.300
on Cloud SQL, specifically
on Cloud SQL's MySQL product.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.250
So as part of the
talk today, we're

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.130
going to cover a little
bit on what Cloud SQL is.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.190
We'll talk about how to scale
Cloud SQL infrastructure, how

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.060
to scale your database instance
up, and how to scale out.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.600
And Stanley then will cover
balancing availability

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.600
and raw MySQL
performance, especially

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.780
in the context of MySQL's
semi-synchronous replication

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.100
technology.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.280
From there, we'll get into
some InnoDB tuning settings

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.309
that we use on Cloud SQL.

00:00:55.309 --> 00:00:56.850
I'll note there that
you're more than

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.560
welcome to snap
picture the slides,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.240
and this video we posted online.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.210
But Cloud SQL actually
posts our tuning parameters

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.850
on GitHub Gist, so we have
those all in one place for you.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.384
So no need to try to write
down some of the settings

00:01:08.384 --> 00:01:09.300
that Stanley mentions.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.150
We'll give you the URL at
the end of the presentation.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.794
The hope here is that
for those of you who

00:01:14.794 --> 00:01:16.710
are Cloud SQL users
today, you learn something

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.210
about scaling Cloud SQL.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.210
And for those of you who
are running MySQL yourself,

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.570
you at least can crib off of
our InnoDB tuning parameters,

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:28.574
as a way to get started more
rapidly with MySQL on GCB.

00:01:28.574 --> 00:01:30.240
And then we'll get
into some next steps,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.470
and kind of wrap up from there.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.400
But first, I want to ask a few
questions to get to know you

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.480
a little bit.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.660
By show of hands, how many
of you are developers?

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.980
We'll call that,
what, 2/3 of the room?

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:45.650
2/3 of the room?

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.737
How many of you are database
administrators, DBAs?

00:01:49.737 --> 00:01:51.070
And maybe a quarter of the room.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:54.800
How many of you
are reluctant DBAs?

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.000
A few more hands there.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.940
OK.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:02.150
And how many managers do
we have in the audience?

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.830
We've got a fair
percentage there.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:04.880
OK, thanks for that.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.660
A couple more questions for you.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.479
How many of you use
MySQL frequently?

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.935
How many are you using
it almost day to day?

00:02:10.935 --> 00:02:11.810
OK, much of the room.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.707
How about PostgreSQL?

00:02:14.707 --> 00:02:16.040
Oh, we've got a few hands there.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:16.540
OK.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.769
And how many of you are
current Cloud SQL users?

00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:20.560
That's pretty good.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.860
All right, thank you.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:22.360
OK.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.270
So format wise, we've got
mics at the front of the room.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.310
Stanley and I are
going to leave time

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.540
at the end of the presentation
for your questions.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.790
So if things are coming into
your mind as we go along,

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.490
please jot them
down, and make sure

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.990
that we get to ask them at
the end of the presentation.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.470
We will also hang around
after the presentation

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:39.910
if you have
additional questions.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.430
And we have members of the
Cloud SQL engineering team here,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.740
the people who actually
built the product, who

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.930
will be able to answer
your questions as well.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.400
So let's jump in.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.980
Cloud SQL is a managed
database service.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:56.350
Our goal is to take mundane
database administration tasks

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.030
and make them much easier.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.160
Cloud SQL is not a DBA.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.040
For developers, we hope that
Cloud SQL makes it easier

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.770
for you to focus a bit more on
development, and a little less

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:07.810
on database administration.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.730
And for DBAs, we
hope you get to do

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.200
something more interesting than
install and patch databases.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.850
I will note that historically,
Cloud SQL has offered MySQL.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:20.800
And when I say we offer
MySQL, we don't just

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.140
offer a MySQL
compatibility layer,

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.750
we offer standard MySQL
standard binaries.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.440
Which means with few exceptions,
if your application works

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.740
with MySQL, it will
work with Cloud SQL.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.460
I'm very happy to note that
as of yesterday, Cloud SQL is

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.305
launching PostgreSQL support.

00:03:38.305 --> 00:03:40.430
And again, this doesn't
mean a compatibility layer.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:44.290
This means standard PostgreSQL
database on Cloud SQL.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.670
So a couple of notes on that.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.650
Because we did have
a fair number of you

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:49.900
in the audience who raised
your hands to my PostgreSQL

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.540
question, and I see a few
familiar faces out there

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.060
from the presentation
yesterday, PostgreSQL

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.530
is launching, as
of yesterday, when

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.960
I say launching I
truly mean launching.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.920
If some of you pull up your
cloud console, some in the room

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.711
will see PostgreSQL right
now, while others will not.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:07.960
As the day progresses, and
certainly into the weekend,

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.540
more and more of you will see
PostgreSQL in your console.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.900
If you are familiar with our
CLI and our APIs, all of you

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.154
can create a PostgreSQL
instance right now.

00:04:16.154 --> 00:04:17.529
So we hope you
get started there.

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:19.870
A few notes on PostgreSQL.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.910
We support up to 32
core and 200 gig plus

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:24.910
instances on PostgreSQL.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:28.570
The same goes for MySQL, our
large instance type, 32 cores

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:30.440
and 208 gigs of RAM.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.580
We also offer inexpensive
development instances,

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.570
starting at a little
less than $10 again,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.350
both for PostgreSQL
and for MySQL.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.630
On the PostgreSQL side,
looking at connectivity,

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:41.860
PostgreSQL today--

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:44.020
Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL--
supports connectivity

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.410
from Compute Engine, from App
Engine flexible environment,

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:49.960
which just went GA
at this conference,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.200
From Google Container
Engine, and generally

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.250
from any client with a
standard PostgreSQL connector.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.400
Two caveats here.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:04:59.950
One is that posts--

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:01.460
or Cloud SQL for
PostgreSQL product

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.160
does not yet
support connectivity

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.260
from App Engine
standard environment,

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.240
which our MySQL product does.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.400
And generally, Cloud
SQL does not yet

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.190
connect with Cloud Functions,
although we're working on that.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.310
Cloud Functions was announced
here at this event as well.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.250
I've listed the
extension support

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:21.250
we've come out with on the
PostgreSQL product at beta.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.820
Extensibility is likely one
of the most powerful features

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.200
that PostgreSQL offers.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.040
We do offer the very
popular PostGIS extension,

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.740
which builds in very
powerful geospatial support

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:33.700
into PostgreSQL.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.080
And you see some others here.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:36.850
That said, you've
got us here, you've

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:38.050
got the Cloud SQL team here.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.716
And we want your
feedback on extensions.

00:05:39.716 --> 00:05:41.377
If you're using
PostgreSQL today,

00:05:41.377 --> 00:05:43.960
you use an extension you don't
see on this slide, let us know.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.850
We also have some
forums online where

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:47.770
you can get that kind
of information to us.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.241
We're all ears when it
comes to extensions.

00:05:50.241 --> 00:05:50.740
OK.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.070
So let's get into Google's
database storage portfolio.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:58.549
The database portfolio at
Google is broad and growing.

00:05:58.549 --> 00:06:01.090
So I think one relevant question
is, where does Cloud SQL fit

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:02.140
into all of this?

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.300
If you start right
most on this slide,

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.340
BigQuery is our data
warehousing product.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.570
We store objects in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:14.380
We have two non-relational
or NoSQL databases,

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.840
in Datastore and Bigtable.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.590
And then you get to
the relational column,

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.860
where Cloud SQL and
Cloud Spanner lives.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.560
If you haven't had a chance
to go to the Spanner talks,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.960
I encourage you to watch the
YouTube recordings of those,

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:27.501
or just get to know
Spanner with some

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:28.790
of the online documentation.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.090
It's a product we just
recently announced.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.840
And left most on the slide
is our App Engine Memcache

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.577
product, which is our
in-memory database product.

00:06:37.577 --> 00:06:39.160
So this kind of leads
to the question,

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.680
which is which of these
products do I pick?

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.450
You may have seen
this flow chart

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:45.190
before in other presentations.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.230
It's just meant to,
at a high level,

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:50.380
kind of guide you to the
right Google database product.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.630
In this session, we
are talking about

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:58.000
structured transactional
relational data that gets you

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.770
to the bottom left
of this slide, where

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:02.860
Cloud SQL and Spanner live
as our relational products.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.910
And then let me just
ask, by a show of hands,

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:07.060
how many of you are
comfortable saying,

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:11.092
I know the difference between
Cloud SQL and Spanner?

00:07:11.092 --> 00:07:12.300
So about a third of the room.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.270
Just very quickly, and
there's much more depth

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:19.359
than I'm going to touch on here,
Cloud SQL is compatibility.

00:07:19.359 --> 00:07:21.150
Again, we're not just
a compatibility layer

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.320
for MySQL and PostgreSQL.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.050
We are offering standard MySQL
and PostgreSQL databases.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:29.580
Cloud SQL, like many
relational database services,

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:32.010
is able to scale
out reads, and we'll

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:33.690
talk about this in
this presentation,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:37.180
but we are not able to scale
out writes past a single node.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:38.937
So one difference
between Cloud SQL,

00:07:38.937 --> 00:07:40.770
from a performance
perspective, and Spanner,

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.770
is that Spanner does
horizontally scale writes.

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:45.980
And that's the horizontal
scalability question

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:47.940
you see in this flowchart.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.440
The other note here is
around that compatibility.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.110
So if you have a
MySQL application,

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.960
a PostgreSQL application,
with very few exceptions

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.490
it will work with Cloud SQL.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.502
Spanner has its own
compatibility model,

00:07:58.502 --> 00:08:00.210
which means if you're
coming with a MySQL

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.340
or PostgreSQL or an
Oracle app, you likely

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:04.980
need to take a look at
changes to that application

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:06.450
so that it works with Spanner.

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.210
Spanner comes with a myriad
of benefits in terms of scale

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.550
and global availability of data.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.655
I encourage you to get
to know it a bit more.

00:08:13.655 --> 00:08:15.030
But for the purposes
of this talk

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:17.760
today, we're going to be
focused squarely on Cloud SQL

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.380
here forward.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:19.880
OK.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:23.257
So I've talked about Cloud
SQL being a managed database

00:08:23.257 --> 00:08:25.090
service, and I think
it's worthwhile to take

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:26.862
a look at what it takes
to run a database.

00:08:26.862 --> 00:08:28.570
It's just a way to
illustrate differences

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.780
between self-managing
a database like MySQL,

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:33.880
and using something
like Cloud SQL.

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.220
And in the end, it all
comes down to trade offs.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:39.429
I, obviously, have a bias as the
product manager for Cloud SQL.

00:08:39.429 --> 00:08:41.110
I love managed database service.

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.140
But there are merits
to both self-managing

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:45.980
and managed database services.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.560
So let's start
with the hardware.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.470
Some of you are today
running on prem.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:52.870
You are thinking about things
like power and cooling,

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:55.510
racking and stacking of servers,
maybe configuring servers

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:57.610
on some vendor's website.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:01.090
Whether you are doing that or
you've offloaded some of that

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.960
to another group in your
company, a colo, a cloud

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.840
provider like Google, I
would encourage all of you

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:10.240
to still think about
server maintenance.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.880
Here at Google, part of our
server maintenance strategy

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:14.222
is live migration.

00:09:14.222 --> 00:09:16.680
You can read a number of blogs
that the Compute Engine team

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.460
has put out on live migration.

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.784
I think it's a really
cool feature with respect

00:09:20.784 --> 00:09:21.700
to server maintenance.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.908
And server maintenance should
be on all of our radar,

00:09:23.908 --> 00:09:26.530
given that it has obvious
implications to our application

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:27.190
stack.

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:30.404
Cloud SQL, by the way, benefits
from live migration as well.

00:09:30.404 --> 00:09:31.570
Let's take a look at the OS.

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:33.130
And this is where
we really start

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:36.370
to see a fork in the road
between self-managing MySQL,

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:39.490
for example, on Compute
Engine here in Google Cloud,

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.740
or using something
like Cloud SQL.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:44.410
On Compute Engine, you
will be installing the OS

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:46.960
or picking an image
with an OS installed,

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.670
patching and
maintaining that OS.

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:52.047
On Cloud SQL, we
mask the OS from you.

00:09:52.047 --> 00:09:53.380
You don't have access to the OS.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:55.210
We take care of
the installation,

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:57.382
the patch is keeping it secure.

00:09:57.382 --> 00:09:59.590
From a database perspective,
in a self-managed world,

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:01.480
obviously you're
installing MySQL.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:04.640
You are patching it on some
regular interval, I hope.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:06.040
And you're setting up backups.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:09.190
Cloud SQL, again, does the
install, does the patching,

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:12.070
and makes backups automatic,
or and also gives you

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:14.380
APIs to call them on demand.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.600
And then for advanced
features, when

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.160
you're looking at
high availability,

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:20.350
those self-managing
MySQL today know

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:22.660
that you're thinking about
things like replication,

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.900
you're looking at tooling to
discover, is my database--

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.160
my master database healthy?

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:28.130
Do I need to failover?

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:30.850
If I do, you're either using
alerts to manually failover,

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.920
having someone paged,
or you've written

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:35.890
scripts that load balance
and failover to another MySQL

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:36.810
instance.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.260
Cloud SQL does this
on your behalf.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:42.490
It's a check-box in Cloud SQL
to set up high availability.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:44.460
We take care of the
replication they'll

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.310
be talking a fair
amount about today,

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:47.920
as well as all the
tooling to health

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.720
monitor and do your failover.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:52.060
The other thing you get with
a managed database service

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.250
is that you get the
backing of Google's team.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.410
When you're
self-managing, your team

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.980
is responsible for keeping
that database system up.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.650
When you move to Cloud SQL,
you lean on Google's team

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:04.300
to do that for you.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.390
Last note, in terms of
this stack, is scaling.

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:10.420
Self-managed world, you
are deciding when to scale,

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:11.300
and how to scale.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:12.110
How do I scale up?

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:13.180
How do I scale out?

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:16.120
In Cloud SQL, we make
scaling easy for you.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:18.610
But unlike other
database products

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:21.280
in the portfolio I
showed you earlier,

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:22.774
Cloud SQL does not auto scale.

00:11:22.774 --> 00:11:24.190
So you, as an
administrator, still

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:26.630
need to decide it's
time for me to scale.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:29.080
Cloud SQL makes scaling
up and out fairly easy,

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:31.690
but you are the one who
triggers that action.

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:34.125
And last, but not
least, is monitoring.

00:11:34.125 --> 00:11:36.250
This is not only things
like application monitoring

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.290
with stacked driver
here in Google Cloud,

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.300
but also answering
questions like,

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:43.120
can I get slow query
logs sent to Pub/Sub?

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.450
Or to cloud logging for easy
consumption by third party

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:48.940
products, like
Splunk, for example.

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.500
Cloud SQL and
managed services do

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:52.234
a lot of that plumbing for you.

00:11:52.234 --> 00:11:53.650
Whereas in the
self-managed world,

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:55.510
you're doing that
of your own accord.

00:11:55.510 --> 00:11:58.720
The bottom line when I look at
self-managing versus managing

00:11:58.720 --> 00:11:59.800
is it's a trade off.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:02.350
You all are making trade offs
with your time day to day.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:05.284
Certain parts of your technology
stack, you want to customize,

00:12:05.284 --> 00:12:06.700
you want to spend
more time there.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:08.575
And that means you're
willing to maintain it.

00:12:08.575 --> 00:12:11.260
Other parts, you may say, I
don't want to maintain it,

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.750
and I'm willing to give up
some of that customization

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:16.300
to avoid the maintenance.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:16.800
OK.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.105
So looking at scaling
Cloud SQL at itself,

00:12:20.105 --> 00:12:21.980
we're going to first
focus on infrastructure.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:25.590
And then we're going to get into
some real details around MySQL.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:27.480
Scaling up, I think
is pretty intuitive

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:28.740
when it comes to database.

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.694
We encourage it
because it works.

00:12:30.694 --> 00:12:32.610
It works as high as you
can scale up, that is,

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:34.830
and it's relatively
straightforward.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.960
In Cloud SQL and
generally in Google Cloud,

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:40.740
it's easy to add CPU cores
and memory to your instances.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:42.960
When you add them
and you click Apply,

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.750
we do take a reboot in
Cloud SQL and on GCE,

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.970
as you scale up CPU and RAM.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:50.730
I think those are pretty
intuitive scale up levers.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:53.250
One less intuitive
way to scale up

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.620
is scaling up
storage performance.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:59.460
In Google Cloud platform,
we scale storage performance

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:00.490
with capacity.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.940
So as you add
capacity to PD-SSD,

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:04.442
you get more IOPS
and measurement

00:13:04.442 --> 00:13:05.400
of storage performance.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:06.990
Same goes with Cloud SQL.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:09.480
You see some numbers here on
the right hand of the slide.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:11.430
Also you see a screen
shot on the left hand

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:12.390
part of the slide.

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:15.590
In Cloud SQL, we show you
a performance calculator

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:19.320
so that as you adjust the size
of your instance number of CPU

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:23.010
cores, for example, and
the capacity of your,

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:26.400
in this case, persistent
disk SSD drive,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:28.560
we show you what you
can expect in terms

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.500
of storage performance and
overall machine performance.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:35.190
In the example I've shown here,
I selected a 32 core machine,

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:37.620
our largest instance
type in Cloud SQL,

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:41.520
and you can see it can deliver
me a maximum of 25,000 IOPS.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.230
That limit has
actually been raised

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:45.590
as of this conference,
as Persistent Disk has

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:46.440
raised its limits.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.200
But a maximum in this
example of 25,000 IOPS.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:50.850
But given that
I've only selected

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.490
600 gigabytes for
my storage capacity,

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:55.620
I will only deliver 18,000 IOPS.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:59.010
So one action I can take
now is increased capacity

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:01.400
of my storage to
max out those IOPS.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:04.320
I'll note here that when
we increase capacity

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.250
of storage, and
thus performance,

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:07.480
this is an online operation.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.480
You need not take
down your database.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.790
In fact, Cloud SQL
has an auto storage

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:14.100
grow feature, if
you check that box,

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:16.260
will watch your
capacity for you.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:19.080
When you need more capacity,
we'll grow your storage device,

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:20.220
and we do so online.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:22.650
The database doesn't go down.

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:24.090
Scaling up, making sense?

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:24.790
Easy enough.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:25.510
OK.

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:29.930
At some point, you say I'm
punishing my single MySQL

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:33.080
instance so much that I need to
consider moving some load off

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:34.670
of it.

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:36.560
As I mentioned at the
beginning, Cloud SQL

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:39.410
makes scaling out reads easy.

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:42.770
We allow you to very simply
create read replicas.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.770
And just by show of hands here,
how many of you in the room

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:48.680
are familiar with differences
between Cloud SQL's

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:54.070
semi-synchronous replication and
its asynchronous replication?

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:57.530
So a number of you are
fairly comfortable with that.

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:01.430
Let me give you the
one minute overview.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.320
semi-synchronous
replication, Cloud SQL

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:05.600
uses for availability.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:08.880
You see in this chart, we see
three total database instances

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:09.380
here.

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:10.910
This is a very
common configuration.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:13.400
I have my master
instance leftmost.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:15.530
I have a failover
replica, the place

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:18.440
I will failover if some problem
happens to my master instance.

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:21.800
What I'm optimizing,
therefore, is data.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:24.140
When I failover, I
want to be very sure

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:26.330
that the last transaction
my application sent

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:28.040
to the master that
was committed,

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:29.920
is indeed in the
failover replicas.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.990
That when I failover,
I have my data intact.

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:36.200
To do that, MySQL incurs
performance overhead.

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:39.230
The master will wait
for the failover replica

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:41.840
not to commit the transaction,
but to acknowledge

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:43.854
that that transaction
is in its relay log.

00:15:43.854 --> 00:15:45.520
So if you imagine the
transaction coming

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.140
from the application,
without a replica,

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:50.760
it goes from the application
to the master instance,

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:53.240
the master instance commits
it, tells the application I've

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:55.190
got it, and the
application is now

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:57.170
free to send
another transaction.

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:59.150
When we add this
failover replica,

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:03.320
the matter takes the
application's transaction,

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:05.240
it waits for the
failover replica

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:07.550
to say I've got it
in my relay log.

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:10.310
Once it does that and commits
the transaction locally,

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:11.670
it gets back to the application.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.330
So you can see the performance
overhead that is incurred here.

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:17.060
The benefit, of course, is
you get data in two places.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.930
In Cloud SQL, we always make
sure your failover replica

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.740
is in a separate
zone, effectively

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:24.170
a failure domain or you can
think of it as a separate data

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:27.590
center, so that if a problem
occurs in one data center zone

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:30.050
A, we can failover to zone B.

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:33.890
This differs from Cloud SQL's
asynchronous replication

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:37.190
in that asynchronous replication
does not require the master

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:40.880
to wait for the replica to
acknowledge the transaction.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.610
So my application can
send a transaction,

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:46.002
the master says, I'm
going to commit it,

00:16:46.002 --> 00:16:47.960
I'm going to make sure
that my replica is aware

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:49.340
that it needs to do
something, but I'm not

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.770
going to wait for that replica
to actually take action

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:53.540
before I get back
to the application.

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:55.400
In this way, you
incur less overhead

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:56.990
with an asynchronous replica.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.780
So asynchronous replicas
Cloud SQL does not

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:03.080
use for failover because there
is a chance it does not have

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.030
the same dataset as the master.

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:06.984
We do use them for
read scale out.

00:17:06.984 --> 00:17:08.900
And in this depiction,
what you're seeing here

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:13.020
is an application is able to
write to the master instance,

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:16.260
and read from any of the
replicas, and of course,

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:17.470
the master instance itself.

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:18.819
So from a scale-up
perspective, what

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:21.152
we've been able to accomplish
here in our application is

00:17:21.152 --> 00:17:23.000
we've moved expensive reads.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.010
For example, ad hoc
queries or reports

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:28.250
that get generated on Friday
afternoon, to a replica,

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:32.360
so that our master instance
can focus more of its cycles on

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:33.342
writes.

00:17:33.342 --> 00:17:34.300
Make sense to everyone?

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:37.340
And we'll get deeper
as we go along.

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:41.960
So I mentioned a few times now,
Cloud SQL makes scaling easy,

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:43.730
but it doesn't auto scale.

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:45.830
You have to make a decision
as to when to scale.

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:48.742
And so that begs
this question, we

00:17:48.742 --> 00:17:50.450
recommend strongly
you know your numbers.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:52.074
When you look at the
Cloud SQL console,

00:17:52.074 --> 00:17:54.570
you see a basic set of stats
that are available to you.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:57.560
We're showing one of
those stats in this chart.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:02.210
We also, as Cloud SQL, turn
on the InnoDB metrics table

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:05.510
that you can query for
really deep MySQL stats.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:08.240
And we encourage you to refer
to both of these in addition

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:11.060
the Stackdriver, as you
make decisions about is it

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:12.440
time to scale up?

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:14.030
Or if I scaled up
as far as I can,

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:17.230
is it now time to
scale my reads out?

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:21.100
With that, we're going to
get into a bit of discussion

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:24.550
on the relationship between
availability and performance.

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:26.860
And especially how availability
and raw performance

00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:27.970
are very much intertwined.

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:29.110
And I'll hand things
over to Stanley.

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:29.890
Thanks, Stanley.

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:31.056
STANLEY FENG: Thanks, Brett.

00:18:31.056 --> 00:18:32.122
Can you guys hear me?

00:18:32.122 --> 00:18:33.590
All right.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:34.360
Cool.

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:37.620
So I can have two topics.

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:40.180
Today, one is about
availability and performance

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:41.770
as shown on this slide.

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:44.080
The other topic is about,
I can talk a little bit

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:46.270
about the InnoDB
[INAUDIBLE] tuning we have

00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:48.190
done to make the Cloud SQL.

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:51.670
MySQL works best on
the GCE platform.

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:54.689
So let's start with this
availability and performance.

00:18:54.689 --> 00:18:56.980
In this section, I'm going
to talk about how do we best

00:18:56.980 --> 00:19:00.890
balance in between replication
lag and the raw performance.

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:02.840
So let's start with
a brief introduction

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.920
in the overview of the
Cloud SQL HA architecture.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.830
So HA here stands for
high availability.

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:11.050
Brett already gave
us a brief overview

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:13.660
of what semi-synchronous
replication is.

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:16.090
So basically, yes,
our HA architecture

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:19.600
is currently viewed on top of
semi-synchronous replication

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:22.955
feature, which is built
in and available in MySQL.

00:19:22.955 --> 00:19:25.750
And as you can see, on this
picture there is a master

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:26.920
and there's a slave.

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:30.610
And both are live and
hot online instances.

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:34.030
And then the master
supports reads and writes.

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:35.630
The slave only supports read.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:37.930
But if the master crash,
slave will take over

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:39.090
and becomes a master.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:41.290
The replication between
these two instances

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:43.810
are done by semi-synchronous
replication,

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:47.470
in the sense that each and
every writes on that master

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:51.190
has to be durably committed
on the relay log of the slave,

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:54.220
before the master can consider
that transaction successful.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:57.910
That part somehow guarantees
durability, in the sense

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:02.320
that a transaction is
persisted on both copies on two

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:03.179
different hosts.

00:20:03.179 --> 00:20:05.220
In this particular example,
as you can see there,

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:07.570
even persisting in
two different zones.

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:10.630
So however, the
semi part comes in.

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:13.150
When the slave picks up
this transaction window,

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:15.010
applies that
particular transaction

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:18.580
to the database of the slave,
it does that asynchronously

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:19.840
in the background.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.510
And generally, it
only has one thread

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:25.010
that's picking up those
transactions from the relay log

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:26.860
and applies to the database.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:29.310
It has only one
thread in MySQL 5.6.

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:33.930
It has an option to enable
multiple sites in MySQL 5.7.

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:36.490
And we haven't enabled
that yet because there

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:40.510
have been some concerns about
reliability and data integrity

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:41.090
issues.

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:45.580
So bottom line, if the incoming
write rate to the master

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:47.860
exceeds the capabilities
of the slave,

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:50.950
that single thread on slave
can apply this transaction,

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:53.100
then you have replication lag.

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:56.250
Those transactions will
be saved in the relay log.

00:20:56.250 --> 00:21:00.820
They are safe, but the slave
is lagging behind the master.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:04.910
So let's take a look
at the replication lag,

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:06.370
and why it matters.

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:07.210
Why?

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:10.000
Because when the
failover happened,

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:14.410
the slave has to apply all those
transactions in the relay log

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:18.090
onto its database, before
it can open up for business.

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:20.680
And, remember in this
high HA architecture,

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:22.840
the slave is actually
hot and online.

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:25.180
If you don't have
any replication lag,

00:21:25.180 --> 00:21:28.240
then the slave should just be
able to take over and flip over

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:29.320
really fast.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:32.920
Now, this connects back to
the concept called RTO, which

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.020
is Recovery Time Objective.

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:41.320
So every serious production
use of my MySQL, the business

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:44.170
have a need to support a
particular recovery time

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:45.120
objective.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.920
In this case that
if the master dies,

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:51.520
the slave has to be up and
running within x seconds.

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:53.590
That's your recovery
time objective.

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:56.350
And so it's therefore
important that you

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:59.890
need to be able to monitor and
control your replication lag

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:04.210
if you want to make sure that
your system can fit and meet

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:06.320
your RTO.

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:09.940
One way to do it is to throttle
your application's write read.

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:11.110
And there is a way to do it.

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:15.640
It's probably a harder way is to
partition and shatter database

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:18.710
and have a different instance.

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:22.555
So let's take a look
at how do you monitor

00:22:22.555 --> 00:22:24.520
in the see replication lag?

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:27.340
So this, I showed
an example here,

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:32.210
which is the SQL command you
can run on the slave side.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:34.120
And basically, on
the slave side,

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:37.480
MySQL maintains a
status variable called

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:40.810
Seconds_Behind_Master, so you
can do a show slave status

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:43.536
and then grab for
Seconds_Behind_Master.

00:22:43.536 --> 00:22:46.450
And so replication lag is
measured in seconds, not

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:49.187
in milliseconds, not in
hours, but in seconds.

00:22:49.187 --> 00:22:50.770
And so here, the
Seconds_Behind_Master

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:53.260
is 1,586 seconds.

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:56.200
That means the slave
is still applying

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:59.650
the transaction that has
happened on the master 1,500

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:00.670
seconds ago.

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:03.220
So it's a pretty bad situation.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:05.720
Well, bad or good,
depending on your RTO.

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:09.580
So here's another view
of the replication lag.

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:11.050
Let's see how--

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:14.510
The chart is a little bit
blurry on those lines,

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:17.110
but I think you can still see
the numbers and the lines.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:21.680
So this is a sysbench
run, sysbench OLTP

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:25.390
run on MySQL 5.6 instance.

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:28.540
And the way we run
this experiment is we

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:30.440
increment a test load--

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:30.940
doubled.

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:33.130
We doubled a test load
each step of the way.

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:35.060
Each step here runs
for 20 minutes.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:39.430
So here, you can see on the
x-axis, as a test thread count,

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:43.450
we run from 1, 2, 4, 8,
all the way to 256 threads.

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:46.170
On the y-axis, is
basically the number,

00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:49.300
the slave reported
seconds behind master.

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:52.340
So you can see on this
graph, when you run the test,

00:23:52.340 --> 00:23:55.720
it ranges from 1 to
8 threads, we are OK.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:58.540
We pretty much don't
have any replication lag,

00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:00.910
it's pretty much close to 0.

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:04.630
Now, at 6 test thread count,
the incoming write rates

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:06.976
start to exceed the
rate the slave can apply

00:24:06.976 --> 00:24:08.350
those transactions,
and you start

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:12.310
to have non-zero replication
lag, and all the way to 256

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:16.030
so that you have a couple
of thousand seconds behind.

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:18.760
One key thing to remember
here, though, on this chart,

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:22.720
is that are you OK to
run at 16 thread count?

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:23.560
Probably not.

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:26.590
Because at that level,
your incoming write rate

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:30.050
is already exceeding,
exceeded your slave--

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:32.360
there's a rate slave can
apply those transaction.

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:35.300
In the sense that if you
keep running on this one,

00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:38.570
this line is going to keep
increasing, keep increasing.

00:24:38.570 --> 00:24:43.080
So keeping that's an
example of replication lag.

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:46.867
And then next slide, I'm going
to talk about how do we monitor

00:24:46.867 --> 00:24:47.700
and to control them?

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:49.180
How do we moderate?

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:52.020
So this one shows
the relationship

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.300
between replication
lag and the transaction

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:55.560
and a raw performance.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:57.390
So previous slides
showed examples

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:00.150
of replication lag,
this one brings the TPS

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:01.360
into the picture.

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:03.810
So the way to read
this picture is

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:06.540
x-axis, again, is a test load.

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:09.180
This is, again,
sysbench OLTP run,

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:11.550
and each step runs
for about 20 minutes.

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:15.720
And we run from one thread
to all the way to 256 thread.

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:17.970
On this chart there are
lines and there is columns.

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:20.340
Columns are for replication lag.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:23.980
And its on the write
axis, right y-axis.

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:25.620
And these are in seconds.

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:29.190
And then so lines are for
TPS, transaction per second.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:32.480
These are for on
the left y-axis.

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:35.950
So and also, you can note
there are two lines here.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:41.970
One is the red, which is
from a non-replicated test.

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:44.190
And then the green one
is for a replicate test.

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:46.140
And one interesting
thing to note

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:49.190
is that because we're using
semi-synchronous replication,

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:52.440
the performance gap between non-
replicate and the replicated,

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:54.300
isn't that big.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:57.420
Because if we are going through
the completely synchronous

00:25:57.420 --> 00:26:00.690
replication, especially
if you're across zone,

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:04.580
you would expect to see much
larger gap on these two lines.

00:26:04.580 --> 00:26:07.880
But because we are using
semi-synchronous replication,

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:10.470
the performance hit
from non-replicated one

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:11.790
isn't that much big.

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:14.520
However, the price
we are paying here

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:17.290
is the replication lag here.

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:22.050
So the way it can read the
chart is for this 32 test thread

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:25.760
count, we are able to
achieve about 200 TPS.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:27.630
And those are the p50 TPS.

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:31.850
But the replication lag, we
can see at the end of this 20

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:35.530
minutes that is 467 seconds.

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:37.020
So what does that mean?

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:41.460
How does that help you on this?

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:42.960
Let's look at the next slide.

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:46.050
So on this slide, it's the same
picture as the previous one.

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:49.110
I marked two
distinct TPS ranges.

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:52.710
The first one is what I
call a sustained TPS range.

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:56.760
So is the sustained TPS range,
it ranges from 0 to 200,

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:59.280
and then you can run
from one test column--

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:01.570
1 test thread to 8 test thread.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:04.960
In this range, your replication
lag is virtually zero.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:09.380
So you can run your application
at this TPS for a very, very

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:11.760
long time without
ever needing to worry

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:14.070
about violating your RTO.

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:17.940
However, when you start
to exceed this range,

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:20.850
then you will start to
accrue your replication lag.

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:23.520
This is what we call a peek TPS.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:25.740
So from time to time,
your application

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:28.050
would like to burst,
would like to exceed

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:29.820
the standard TPS range.

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:31.210
That's what the TPS--

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:33.450
peak TPS is.

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:36.100
So the way you want to
model your application

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:38.280
is that you would
run your workload,

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:41.190
and you create a charge
similar to this one.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.410
And then you also
consider how long you

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:46.060
would allow that first to go.

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:49.770
And then you can see
if the replication

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:55.470
lag at the end of your
burst is below your RTO.

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:58.320
For example, if you're RTO
is 500, in this case, then

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:02.670
that means your peak TPS
can be as high as 200

00:28:02.670 --> 00:28:04.380
for up to 20 minutes.

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:06.550
So on the lower right
corner of the slide,

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:08.280
you can see a
little formula here

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:11.910
is that the peak TPS times
the duration you want

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:15.720
to burst your TPS to, times
your estimated replication lag

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:19.770
growth rate, should be
controlled under your RTO.

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:21.620
So why do we do this?

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:23.880
How does this thing help you?

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.430
The most important
information this can give you

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:29.040
is to help you do
capacity planning.

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:30.640
So imagine you have
your application.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:33.210
Your application has some
kind of write workload.

00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:36.290
You can run this write
workload, varying its load,

00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:40.600
and then collect replication
lag and the TPS numbers.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:43.920
And then you can also
get a good estimate

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:47.520
of how much burst you want to
give to the application for how

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:49.080
long, during our business day.

00:28:49.080 --> 00:28:52.050
Or during the previous runs.

00:28:52.050 --> 00:28:54.450
So when you find
all this information

00:28:54.450 --> 00:28:57.030
that you can plan
properly, first, you

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:58.860
can consider a threat
to your application

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:00.630
to make sure that
when it bursts,

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:04.110
it doesn't burst above
certain TPS level.

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:08.640
Second, if you really,
really want that many TPS,

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:11.610
then you also want
to stick to your RTO.

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:13.890
Then the next natural
choice is probably

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:16.500
to shatter and
partition your database,

00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:22.530
and have another one to serve
and divide your write traffic.

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:25.600
So OK.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:30.680
That is the section for the
availability and performance.

00:29:30.680 --> 00:29:33.780
So the next section, I'm going
to go over some of the Cloud

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:37.130
SQL specific InnoDB tuning
we have done, to make sure

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:42.160
MySQL works better
on the GC platform.

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:46.120
So for Cloud SQL, we have
tried to tune various InnoDB

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:48.160
parameters, because InnoDB is--

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:51.490
MySQL, is a very established
and a mature product.

00:29:51.490 --> 00:29:54.340
And it was designed for
bare medal hardware,

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:56.440
and later was also
the default set

00:29:56.440 --> 00:29:59.590
of the InnoDB parameters,
what made in such a way

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:03.640
that the database can run safely
on all sorts of platforms.

00:30:03.640 --> 00:30:06.430
So, in order to make
in InnoDB and MySQL

00:30:06.430 --> 00:30:09.580
work best for GCE, which
as a cloud environment,

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:11.760
has its unique
characteristics, for example,

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:14.900
for Persistent Disk, actually
its write performance

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:18.040
and write latency is
better than reads.

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:22.000
And also, was a new persistent
disk IOPS and the bandwidth

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:22.680
great.

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:26.780
And actually, read has
more bandwidth than writes

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:28.160
on some of the machine types.

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:32.360
So there are all kinds of
clouds unique and properties

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:37.610
that makes it almost required
to tune the InnoDB in a way

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:40.540
that it could work best on
the Google Cloud platform.

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:43.550
So but I would like to start
with one quick note here,

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.750
is all tunings and
the settings described

00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:48.640
here are subject to change.

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:51.250
So view this section as more
like for your information,

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:53.950
for your reference, in
case it also helps you.

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:56.250
If you want to, you can
run your own MySQL instance

00:30:56.250 --> 00:30:58.450
on our GCE platform.

00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:00.410
We are constantly
working on this,

00:31:00.410 --> 00:31:02.890
and we will constantly
be making improvements

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:06.520
based on new information and
new developments happening

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:08.770
on the underlying
operating system.

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:12.010
So we will change those
parameters as we see fit,

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:15.280
and sometimes we may even decide
to revert when some of those

00:31:15.280 --> 00:31:18.440
to default if new
information warrants such.

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:21.100
So keep that in mind.

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:22.780
And I don't think
there's any reason

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:26.060
to take a hard dependency
on these particular values.

00:31:26.060 --> 00:31:27.550
So let's start
with the first one.

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:31.090
InnoDB [INAUDIBLE], which
is one almost everyone

00:31:31.090 --> 00:31:33.910
tunes when they write an
InnoDB on their hardware.

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:37.030
So it's critical for
performance for reads mostly,

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:39.220
and maybe also for writes.

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:42.560
So it needs to be set according
to the available RAM on the VM.

00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:45.760
And standard
recommendation is about 80%

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:47.710
of the total RAM of the VM.

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:52.510
So what we set it to is
here, the wall of text.

00:31:52.510 --> 00:31:55.300
There's many text here, but
just remember two numbers--

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:57.230
92% and 80%.

00:31:57.230 --> 00:32:02.260
So we take 92% of the total
amount of RAM of the VM,

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:04.660
and assign, give it to MySQL.

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:07.570
And then we take 80% of
the MySQL's RAM to InnoDB

00:32:07.570 --> 00:32:08.410
buffer pool.

00:32:08.410 --> 00:32:10.120
Now there are a bunch
of caveats here,

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:14.020
because on smaller VMs for
for m1-standard-4 and below,

00:32:14.020 --> 00:32:16.960
and even for my
core and the small.

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:20.830
Those little VMs, we have
to tune down those numbers

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:23.200
to make sure they can run
safely and are reliable,

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:24.520
they don't crash.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:29.050
So for us, for those who are
interested in doing performance

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:32.680
work, if the data
m1-standard-8 and above,

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:34.430
then these two
numbers will apply.

00:32:34.430 --> 00:32:36.340
And we actually
highly recommend you

00:32:36.340 --> 00:32:41.530
guys to run 16 core or above for
performance, heavy performance

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:43.570
intense workload.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:44.830
It's not an upsell.

00:32:44.830 --> 00:32:47.500
And it's really an
honest suggestion

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:49.460
for many, many reasons.

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:51.760
For example, just
recently Persistent Disk

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:55.570
announced higher IOPS and
the bandwidth offering.

00:32:55.570 --> 00:32:58.180
And the number of cores
actually played a role

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:01.210
on the underlying disk
capability you can get.

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:02.530
Not just the sites.

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:06.830
For six 16 core machines
so you can get 25,000 IOPS.

00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:11.200
And 480 megabytes reads
and 240 megabytes writes.

00:33:11.200 --> 00:33:13.870
So those are not available
in lower core machines,

00:33:13.870 --> 00:33:16.580
even if you buy at
very, very large disk.

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:19.480
So 16 core and above,
we highly recommend

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:20.710
for performance workload.

00:33:20.710 --> 00:33:23.395
Now, next set of
parameters, which is buffer

00:33:23.395 --> 00:33:24.850
pool initialization.

00:33:24.850 --> 00:33:27.370
We enable buffer pool
persistence at shutdown

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:30.070
and the buffer pool
preload at startup.

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:32.530
So this allows us to
pre-warm the cache

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:34.500
and to reduce
performance variability

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:37.820
in between across DB reboots.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:40.630
Pre-warm cache is better
than randomly warming them up

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:42.310
during your production workload.

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:47.560
Next one.

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:48.460
Redo log size.

00:33:48.460 --> 00:33:50.990
So this is the most
important parameter.

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:53.320
I think one of the most
important parameters

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:55.260
for write heavy workload.

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:58.990
Default is 48 megabytes,
which is really too small.

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:01.870
512 megabytes is what
we set, and we set it

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:05.380
to two log files, each
of which 512 megabytes.

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:09.230
So in total, you get 1
gigabyte worth of redo logs.

00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:12.659
So it helps absolve
incoming write spikes.

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:15.610
I would talk a lot more
about what I mean here

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:17.820
by sync flash state.

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:24.110
So next one is networking.

00:34:24.110 --> 00:34:26.440
Networking here always
tuned to two settings.

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:31.480
One is to max_allowed_packet to
32 megabytes from 4 megabyte,

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:33.380
and the other one
is max_connections.

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:41.199
So max_connections to 4,000.

00:34:41.199 --> 00:34:43.500
So max_allowed_packet
allows your kind

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:45.429
to sending large
queries, in case

00:34:45.429 --> 00:34:47.710
you want to insert
a big BLOB column.

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:49.674
Or you have very long
strings in your query

00:34:49.674 --> 00:34:51.940
that would be supported.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:55.850
So max_connections
is self-explanatory.

00:34:55.850 --> 00:34:57.840
So InnoDB metrics monitoring.

00:34:57.840 --> 00:35:02.270
So there is a set of InnoDB
metrics in the information

00:35:02.270 --> 00:35:03.530
schema table.

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:05.390
Those are actually
pretty useful.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:07.610
The default is
actually not enabled.

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:10.340
So we enabled all
type of those metrics

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:12.089
in that particular table.

00:35:12.089 --> 00:35:14.630
Now there's a difference between
that table and a performance

00:35:14.630 --> 00:35:15.130
schema.

00:35:15.130 --> 00:35:17.360
Performance schema
is pretty heavy,

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:21.410
and comes with not
so ideal overhead.

00:35:21.410 --> 00:35:24.350
But for this particular table,
the overhead is trivial,

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:26.570
and the information it
provides is valuable.

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:28.850
So we turn it on,
and it turns out

00:35:28.850 --> 00:35:31.580
to be very useful for our
investigation and debugging

00:35:31.580 --> 00:35:33.590
purpose.

00:35:33.590 --> 00:35:34.350
OK.

00:35:34.350 --> 00:35:36.960
So for the next
set of slides, I'm

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:40.350
going to talk a little bit
about dirty page flushes.

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:44.780
So for InnoDB tuning,
for you to tune writes,

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:47.700
to tune dirty page flush
and to make it properly,

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:50.100
and to make it
adjusted and adapt

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:53.500
to your incoming write row,
is really, really important.

00:35:53.500 --> 00:35:56.220
So let's first start with
a little bit of overview

00:35:56.220 --> 00:35:57.600
of what dirty page is.

00:35:57.600 --> 00:36:01.310
And it has to start with the
way how InnoDB implement writes.

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:04.890
A write has to happen in two
places concurrently in InnoDB.

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:07.530
First, it has to be
persistent in the redo log.

00:36:07.530 --> 00:36:11.640
Second, the write will be made
into an in-memory data page,

00:36:11.640 --> 00:36:13.070
which is in the buffer pool.

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:16.360
And once the writes is
written in the data page,

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:17.640
it's called a dirty page.

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:19.100
And then at the
background, InnoDB

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:22.440
has a mechanism called
Adaptive Flushing, that

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:24.690
will appear [INAUDIBLE]
flush those dirty pages

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:26.580
into the on-disk data file.

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:30.000
Only after the 30 pages are
flushed to the disk file,

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.730
to the data file, then the
content in the redo log

00:36:32.730 --> 00:36:33.840
can be reclaimed.

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:36.930
And the space in the redo
log can be three to four

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:38.490
subsequent writes.

00:36:38.490 --> 00:36:42.944
So that's the way
InnoDB handles writes.

00:36:42.944 --> 00:36:44.360
Here are the two
settings we tuned

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:48.540
to make the flush a little
bit faster and more capable.

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:50.790
One is called
innodb_flush_method,

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:52.320
we change that to O_DIRECT.

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:55.740
Which makes the InnoDB to open
the data file in O_DIRECT mode,

00:36:55.740 --> 00:37:00.660
so each and every write
will hit the disk directly

00:37:00.660 --> 00:37:02.730
without depending on
the subsequent fsync.

00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:05.910
So it makes using
fsync really fast.

00:37:05.910 --> 00:37:08.760
The second one is
innodb_io_capacity.

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:10.470
The default is only 200.

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:13.670
So this is a number that feeds
into the Adaptive Flushing

00:37:13.670 --> 00:37:17.210
algorithm, and tells it
how many dirty pages it can

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:19.410
flush per iteration.

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:21.290
There are many, many
other parameters,

00:37:21.290 --> 00:37:24.100
but this one is one of
the most important ones.

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:26.640
So it has to tie into
the underlying IO

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:28.640
capacity of the block device.

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:31.185
And our persistent
disk, we can support up

00:37:31.185 --> 00:37:33.470
to 15,000, 20,000 IOPS.

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:35.460
And so if we change
that to 5,000,

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:37.860
it turns out to be
pretty well for us.

00:37:40.450 --> 00:37:43.270
So the next set of
parameters that we tuned,

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:46.750
which is currently
for MySQL 5.7 only,

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:48.850
and it's still in the
process of being rolled out

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:52.020
to production, which will
be available everywhere

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:53.620
probably in a few weeks.

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:58.720
Those are really, really for
the tuning for a problem called

00:37:58.720 --> 00:37:59.950
sync flush point.

00:37:59.950 --> 00:38:02.050
Remember, I just
described how InnoDB

00:38:02.050 --> 00:38:06.310
does writes to the redo
log, and to the dirty page,

00:38:06.310 --> 00:38:08.140
and then delivery gets flushed.

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:11.110
Once it's flushed, the redo
log space gets reclaimed.

00:38:11.110 --> 00:38:12.700
Now think about this.

00:38:12.700 --> 00:38:14.650
If the income incoming
write rate far

00:38:14.650 --> 00:38:17.830
exceeded the rate InnoDB
can flush dirty page,

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:19.450
then your redo
log space is going

00:38:19.450 --> 00:38:21.850
to grow, grow, grow to a point.

00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:24.310
Usually it's about
80% of your limit.

00:38:24.310 --> 00:38:27.190
Then InnoDB gets
into a panic mode.

00:38:27.190 --> 00:38:29.430
So-called panic mode
is called sync flush.

00:38:29.430 --> 00:38:32.860
It means that it will not
accept your next write until it

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:36.100
can find the oldest dirty
page, flush it to the disk,

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:37.910
flush it to the
data file, reclaim

00:38:37.910 --> 00:38:40.750
some space from
the redo log, then

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:42.470
they will accept
your new writes.

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:46.010
So from your application level,
you will see on your TPS chart,

00:38:46.010 --> 00:38:48.445
this forms something called
a stop-and-go pattern.

00:38:48.445 --> 00:38:51.180
Your stop, your TPS will drop.

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:53.900
And then you could go
back up because the redo

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:55.270
logs has more space.

00:38:55.270 --> 00:38:58.100
And then you will see a huge
fluctuation of your TPS.

00:38:58.100 --> 00:39:00.696
It will hurt your
capacity planning,

00:39:00.696 --> 00:39:02.320
it will hurt your
front and application

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:04.861
because it doesn't know how to
set timeout when this happens.

00:39:04.861 --> 00:39:07.970
And things will just randomly
time out and will fail.

00:39:07.970 --> 00:39:10.060
So here is a set
of four settings

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:12.800
we tuned to help and
tackle this problem.

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:16.456
And they attacked this
problem from different angles.

00:39:16.456 --> 00:39:19.080
For first one, makes
the flush more smart.

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:21.460
It always tried to
flash a contiguous

00:39:21.460 --> 00:39:24.160
region of 1 megabyte
of data rather than

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:26.350
a bunch of random writes.

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:29.890
The second one makes a flush
do more work for iteration.

00:39:29.890 --> 00:39:34.050
It was scan all pages
in the [INAUDIBLE] tree.

00:39:34.050 --> 00:39:37.510
And the third one makes
the flush respond faster

00:39:37.510 --> 00:39:38.900
to the incoming load.

00:39:38.900 --> 00:39:43.150
And the fourth one increases
the parallelism of the flush.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:46.142
So here's sync flush
problem [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:46.142 --> 00:39:47.100
just described earlier.

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:48.760
It was [AUDIO OUT] still.

00:39:48.760 --> 00:39:51.700
This is a sysbench step
load function each.

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:54.510
Step is 2x the load
of the previous one.

00:39:54.510 --> 00:39:57.160
And what's plotted
here on the y-axis

00:39:57.160 --> 00:39:59.630
is the flush lag in bytes.

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:02.950
So remember, we have 1
gigabyte was of redo log space,

00:40:02.950 --> 00:40:05.500
80% is about 800 megabytes.

00:40:05.500 --> 00:40:07.480
Here is about 800 megabytes.

00:40:07.480 --> 00:40:09.780
So as you can see,
when we run the test

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:13.330
at 128 threads of
sysbench, it quickly--

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:15.730
the flush lag, it
quickly hits the ceiling,

00:40:15.730 --> 00:40:19.510
hits the point where InnoDB
gets into the sync flush state.

00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:21.880
And InnoDB start the
stop-and-go mode,

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:24.580
and you cannot go
higher anymore.

00:40:24.580 --> 00:40:28.970
So if you-- I can show you the
TPS chart in just two slides.

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:30.400
The TPS looks horrible.

00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:32.810
And it will just up
and down, up and down.

00:40:32.810 --> 00:40:36.340
So after the tune in, you
see that's the after picture

00:40:36.340 --> 00:40:39.070
that we successfully
suppressed the growth

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:41.240
rate of the flush lag.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:44.320
That at 128 thread
and 250 thread,

00:40:44.320 --> 00:40:47.530
it is kept away from
the sync flush state.

00:40:47.530 --> 00:40:53.210
But at 256, it still hits
a red line occasionally,

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:55.300
and there's still
more turning to do.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:58.390
So here's an end effect
of the tuned versus

00:40:58.390 --> 00:41:00.910
untuned at 128 threads.

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:05.820
So if you remember the previous
sync flush and the flush lag

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:09.820
chart at 128 threads,
shortly after test start,

00:41:09.820 --> 00:41:11.710
we hit the sync flush
state, and then you

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:13.930
can see this is a TPS chart.

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:18.520
It went as low as 400, and
then it goes back up to 2400.

00:41:18.520 --> 00:41:20.890
And then goes up and
down and up and down.

00:41:20.890 --> 00:41:24.310
So [INAUDIBLE], the
tuning that was applied,

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:27.380
at least it gives you a
very-- a pretty stable

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:30.100
lower bound of 1,200 to 1,200.

00:41:30.100 --> 00:41:32.030
You know, upper bound
it still shoots up,

00:41:32.030 --> 00:41:34.400
and it gets-- you can still
get better performance,

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:38.390
but the key point here is that
as a predictable and reliable

00:41:38.390 --> 00:41:40.660
lower bound of the performance.

00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:42.670
Now with that, I'm going
to turn the mic back

00:41:42.670 --> 00:41:44.430
to Brett to talk
about next steps.

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:46.180
BRETT HESTERBERG: Thank
