00:43:57.140 --> 00:44:00.560
When we talk about optimizing
performance and availability,

00:44:00.560 --> 00:44:01.830
some key points to remember.

00:44:01.830 --> 00:44:05.960
So Cloud SQL uses MySQL's
semi-synchronous replication

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:08.300
technology for
high availability.

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:11.840
What this means is
consider your workload,

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:14.240
and how it might
influence the replication

00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:18.140
lag that Stanley talked about
where replication lag can

00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:19.260
affect your failover time.

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:21.110
It can affect your availability.

00:44:21.110 --> 00:44:23.360
You will not lose
data, or the goal

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:25.910
of semi-synchronous replication
is to ensure data durability

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:26.900
across zones.

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:29.900
But there is that
RTO implication

00:44:29.900 --> 00:44:31.240
that Stanley pointed out.

00:44:31.240 --> 00:44:34.770
The gist here for you, is if
you have a very bursty workload,

00:44:34.770 --> 00:44:36.970
you are pretty
unconstrained even when

00:44:36.970 --> 00:44:38.630
using semi-synchronous
replication.

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:41.990
Stanley showed you the two
lines, a MySQL database

00:44:41.990 --> 00:44:44.540
instance without replication,
which is essentially as

00:44:44.540 --> 00:44:46.100
fast as MySQL can go.

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:48.560
It's just doing
its work locally.

00:44:48.560 --> 00:44:51.590
Versus a replication
pair, a master instance

00:44:51.590 --> 00:44:52.970
with a failover replica.

00:44:52.970 --> 00:44:55.010
And the lines tracked
pretty closely.

00:44:55.010 --> 00:44:57.240
What that means is
for a bursty workload,

00:44:57.240 --> 00:44:58.910
you're going to get
a lot of performance

00:44:58.910 --> 00:45:00.800
out of your replication pair.

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:03.290
And you'll give
your replication--

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:05.150
your failover replica,
rather, some time

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:07.550
to catch up if it gets
behind the master.

00:45:07.550 --> 00:45:10.910
If instead your workload is
not bursty, if it is consistent

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:15.320
and you were driving inserts and
updates on a consistent basis,

00:45:15.320 --> 00:45:17.420
take a look at what
Stanley showed with respect

00:45:17.420 --> 00:45:19.550
to a sustainable TPS.

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:21.830
If you start to get
a replica behind,

00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:25.040
and you continue to
write inserts, updates,

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:27.320
the replica can go further
and further behind,

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:30.350
and you may have to throttle
from the application side.

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:32.990
I think those were the
two takeaways, in my mind,

00:45:32.990 --> 00:45:34.310
from those charts.

00:45:34.310 --> 00:45:37.010
The numbers will surely
change in the future.

